The underlying set of the finite step from a single state on one symbol equals the automaton's step from that state.

The proof follows the expression and the state. For the universal language and for a singleton word both sides are the singleton of the deterministic step. For an intersection the underlying set of a product of steps is the product of the underlying sets, by the inductive hypotheses. For a concatenation, a left state reduces by the left step law to an epsilon-closure: if the step meets an accept state of the first factor, both directions of the closure law match the union of the left-injected stepped states and the right-injected start states of the second factor, and if not, the right-hand disjunct of the closure would exhibit such a meeting, so only the left-injected states remain. A right state reduces by the right step law to a closure whose first disjunct is the right-injected step of the second factor and whose second disjunct is impossible.
